---
import KnowledgeArticle from '../../../components/knowledge/KnowledgeArticle.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is operational intelligence the same as business intelligence?', a: 'No. Business intelligence is mainly about reporting and analysing what has happened, usually from a warehouse refreshed on a schedule. Operational intelligence analyses activity as it happens so that people can act while it still matters. The <a href="/resources/business-observability-vs-bi/">BI comparison guide</a> covers the reporting side in more depth.' },
  { q: 'Is operational intelligence about IT operations?', a: 'The term is used in both senses. In IT it often means analysing machine data from servers and applications. In a business context it means analysing operational events such as orders, cases, shipments and payments. This article uses the business sense.' },
  { q: 'What data does operational intelligence need?', a: 'Timely business events from the systems that run operations, with enough context (which customer, which order, which stage, which team) to aggregate them meaningfully and to route an alert to the right owner.' },
  { q: 'How does operational intelligence relate to business observability?', a: 'Operational intelligence is mostly about seeing and alerting on current activity. Business observability adds an explicit model of how the parts of the business depend on one another, so that a change can be explained and its downstream effects traced.' },
];
