>
  <h2>How does operational intelligence work?</h2>
  <p>An operational intelligence setup typically has four layers:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Capture</strong>: business events stream in from operational systems, or are read frequently, rather than being loaded into a warehouse once a night.</li>
    <li><strong>Correlate</strong>: events are enriched with context and joined, so that "payment failed" is linked to the order, the customer, the payment route and the time.</li>
    <li><strong>Analyse</strong>: rolling measures, thresholds, expected ranges and simple rules run continuously over the stream.</li>
    <li><strong>Act</strong>: when something deviates, a person is alerted, a case is opened or a workflow is triggered.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Operational intelligence vs business intelligence</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Business intelligence</th><th>Operational intelligence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Main question</td><td>What happened, and how did we perform?</td><td>What is happening now, and does it need action?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Scheduled refreshes, often daily</td><td>Continuous or near continuous</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grain</td><td>Aggregated for reporting</td><td>Individual events and short windows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typical users</td><td>Analysts, finance, leadership reviews</td><td>Operations managers and team leads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typical output</td><td>Reports and dashboards</td><td>Alerts, live views and triggered actions</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Both are necessary. BI remains the right place for period reporting, planning and historical analysis.</p>

  <h2>Common uses</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Service commitments</strong>: spotting open cases that are on course to miss a resolution target while there is still time to prioritise them.</li>
    <li><strong>Payment operations</strong>: noticing a rise in declines on one payment route within minutes rather than at month end.</li>
    <li><strong>Fulfilment</strong>: seeing that orders are not being picked fast enough to make a carrier cut-off.</li>
    <li><strong>Queues and capacity</strong>: watching arrivals against completion capacity at each stage of a back-office process.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where operational intelligence falls short</h2>
  <p>Operational intelligence is good at telling you that a number has moved. It is often weaker at three things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Explaining why.</strong> An alert on declined payments says nothing about the release, configuration change or supplier issue behind it.</li>
    <li><strong>Seeing consequences.</strong> A delay in verification affects activation, billing, cash and customer contacts. Alerts on each metric arrive separately, to different teams, with no link between them.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoiding alert fatigue.</strong> Fixed thresholds fire at predictable quiet times and miss real deviations at busy ones, so people learn to ignore them. Expected ranges that account for seasonality help.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Illustrative example</strong>
    Three alerts fire on the same morning: verification wait time is up, first invoices are down and "where is my account" contacts are rising. Each goes to a different team. Seen together, they are one problem with one origin.
  </div>

  <h2>How it relates to business observability</h2>
  <p>Business observability builds on operational intelligence by adding an explicit model of the business: the entities, flows, dependencies, objectives and owners that connect one signal to another. The goal shifts from "alert when a number moves" to "understand what changed, what it affects and who should act".</p>
  <p>Panoptes is designed to provide that model through the <a href="/platform/business-graph/">Business Graph</a>, surface meaningful change with <a href="/platform/anomaly-detection/">Anomaly Detection</a> using expected ranges rather than fixed thresholds, and bring the result into one ranked operating view in the <a href="/platform/command-center/">Command Center</a>.</p>
</KnowledgeArticle>
